      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "RATIONALE",
          "content": "Although trigger point injection is known as an easy and low-risk procedure, it is contraindicated to patients with hemorrhagic disorders or who regularly take anticoagulants/antiplatelets. However, taking clopidogrel is not a defined contraindication to this low-risk procedure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "PATIENT CONCERNS",
          "content": "The chief complaint of a 76-year old woman regularly taking clopidogrel was low back and left buttock pain which prolonged for several years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "DIAGNOSES",
          "content": "The patient was diagnosed with L4-5 and L5-S1 spinal stenosis at the orthopedics department and was referred for lumbar spinal epidural steroid injection."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "She was treated with trigger point injection."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "OUTCOMES",
          "content": "Three hours after the injection, she complained motor weakness and pain in the injection area. A hematoma on left gluteus medium muscle was detected with ultrasonography and ultrasound-guided needle aspiration was accomplished to relieve the symptom."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LESSONS",
          "content": "Trigger point injection for patients taking clopidogrel should be done with a caution to prevent such complication."
        }
      ]},
